  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1041 PM MDT Thu Jul 23 2026\n\nFRIDAY AND SATURDAY:\n\nStorm coverage is expected to be much less on Friday than\nprevious days as a shortwave exits to the east taking the deeper\nmoisture with it and the high pressure center meanders back to\nthe west, allowing a drier air mass to filter into the area from\nthe northwest. This will only be temporary mainly for Friday and\nSaturday with high temperatures rising back to triple digits for\nlower elevation valleys. Even though moisture content will be\nless than previous days with the deepest monsoonal plume to our\nwest over Nevada and western Utah once again, we should still\nhold onto enough moisture to recycle it as it remains trapped\nunder the high, resulting in daily afternoon convection over the\nhigh terrain. While widespread flooding concerns will be\nlimited, we will still see some stronger storms capable of heavy\nrain and resulting in an isolated risk of flash flooding over\nburn scars, already saturated soils and areas of steep terrain.\n\nSUNDAY INTO NEXT WEEK:\n\nThe deeper moisture returns beginning Sunday but more so Monday\nthrough middle of next week as the high shifts eastward,\nallowing the deeper moisture to shift over the western slope\nwith southerly flow on tap for a more favorable trajectory. PWAT\nanomalies will go from a reduced 120 to 140 percent of normal \nFriday and Saturday, to 160 to 180+ percent of normal by early \nto mid next week. This should result in more widespread daily\nstorm coverage as well Sunday through at least mid next week. We\nshould see continued heightened potential for heavy rain and\nflooding concerns especially over burn scars, vulnerable terrain\nand where training storms occur. Our high temperatures should\ndial back some with this increasing moisture, at least not as\nhot with triple digit heat with warm overnight lows as well.\nModels are indicating a tropical system moving off the Baja\nwestward out into the Pacific around the high, which could draw\nsome additional moisture northward during that Monday to\nWednesday timeframe.",
